\section*{Crouzeix's Conjecture}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 325; 2026-09-26), the asserted result is that finite matrices obey the sharp constant-two numerical-range bound for matrix-valued polynomials in every coefficient size, including degenerate numerical ranges; In manuscript 2 (family 325; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that the sharp complete constant-two numerical-range estimate holds for bounded operators without restrictions on Hilbert-space dimension or separability, also for the specified holomorphic and rational calculus.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 325; 2026-09-26), exterior conformal coordinates produce Faber coefficient estimates and a contractive correction kernel. Parseval separation of positive and negative modes controls amplified singular vectors, and enclosing-domain limits include point and segment numerical ranges.

For manuscript 2 (family 325; 2026-09-23), for finite matrices, an optimal positive metric makes the conformal disk coordinate contractive. Endpoint extremal data yield a common continuous boundary density whose ordered comparison forces condition number at most two; domain approximation and finite compressions extend the bound to general operators.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting results are manuscript 1 (family 325; 2026-09-26), manuscript 2 (family 325; 2026-09-23). The manuscript also gives additional strength beyond the requested conclusion; that excess is not required for this problem's solved-claim label.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 325; 2026-09-26), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: finite matrices obey the sharp constant-two numerical-range bound for matrix-valued polynomials in every coefficient size, including degenerate numerical ranges.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

For manuscript 2 (family 325; 2026-09-23), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: the sharp complete constant-two numerical-range estimate holds for bounded operators without restrictions on Hilbert-space dimension or separability, also for the specified holomorphic and rational calculus.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{CompleteCrouzeix.lean}, \texttt{CrouzeixHilbert.lean}, \texttt{DirectCrouzeix.lean}, \texttt{HilbertCrouzeix.lean}, \texttt{StructuralCrouzeix.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.CompleteCrouzeix.main}, \texttt{OAI.CrouzeixHilbert.hilbert}, \texttt{OAI.DirectCrouzeix.complete\_crouzeix}, \texttt{OAI.DirectCrouzeix.uniform\_sharpness}, \texttt{OAI.HilbertCrouzeix.exists\_finite\_compression}, \texttt{OAI.HilbertCrouzeix.numericalClosure\_geometry}, \texttt{OAI.HilbertCrouzeix.rational\_denominator\_isUnit}, \texttt{OAI.HilbertCrouzeix.zeroConclusion}, \texttt{OAI.StructuralCrouzeixReference.challenge}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

